{"step_index":108,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-03T23:47:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-04T05:17:54+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-04T05:17:54+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/notebooklm-py/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 709\nTotal Bytes: 42335\nShowing lines 1 to 709\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\r\n2: name: notebooklm\r\n3: description: Complete API for Google NotebookLM - full programmatic access including features not in the web UI. Create notebooks, add sources, generate all artifact types, download in multiple formats. Activates on explicit /notebooklm or intent like \"create a podcast about X\"\r\n4: ---\r\n5: \r\n6: # NotebookLM Automation\r\n7: \r\n8: Complete programmatic access to Google NotebookLMâ€”including capabilities not exposed in the web UI. Create notebooks, add sources (URLs, YouTube, PDFs, audio, video, images), chat with content, generate all artifact types, and download results in multiple formats.\r\n9: \r\n10: ## Installation\r\n11: \r\n12: **From PyPI (Recommended for AI agents â€” Python-version-aware):**\r\n13: ```bash\r\n14: pip install \"notebooklm-py[browser]\"   # mandatory; errors must propagate\r\n15: \r\n16: # [cookies] (rookiepy) is optional and known to FAIL TO BUILD on Python 3.13+.\r\n17: # Skip it deliberately on 3.13+ rather than swallowing the error â€” that lets\r\n18: # *real* install failures (typos, network, PyPI outages) surface for the agent.\r\n19: if python -c \"import sys; sys.exit(0 if sys.version_info < (3, 13) else 1)\"; then\r\n20:     pip install \"notebooklm-py[cookies]\"   # errors propagate\r\n21: else\r\n22:     echo \"Skipping [cookies] on Python 3.13+ (rookiepy unavailable). Use 'notebooklm login' interactively.\"\r\n23: fi\r\n24: ```\r\n25: \r\n26: > Full install matrix (extras, headless servers, contributor flow): [Installation guide on GitHub](https://github.com/teng-lin/notebooklm-py/blob/main/docs/installation.md).\r\n27: \r\n28: **From GitHub (use latest release tag, NOT main branch):**\r\n29: ```bash\r\n30: # Get the latest release tag (requires curl + jq)\r\n31: if ! command -v jq >/dev/null; then\r\n32:     echo \"jq is required to read the latest release tag\" >&2\r\n33:     exit 1\r\n34: fi\r\n35: LATEST_TAG=$(\r\n36:     curl -fsSL https://api.github.com/repos/teng-lin/notebooklm-py/releases/latest |\r\n37:     jq -r '.tag_name'\r\n38: )\r\n39: # Includes [browser] so the interactive `notebooklm login` flow works.\r\n40: pip install \"notebooklm-py[browser] @ git+https://github.com/teng-lin/notebooklm-py@${LATEST_TAG}\"\r\n41: ```\r\n42: \r\n43: âš ï¸ **DO NOT install from main branch** (`pip install git+https://github.com/teng-lin/notebooklm-py`). The main branch may contain unreleased/unstable changes. Always use PyPI or a specific release tag, unless you are testing unreleased features.\r\n44: \r\n45: **Skill install methods:**\r\n46: \r\n47: - `notebooklm skill install` installs this skill into the supported local agent directories managed by the CLI.\r\n48: - `npx skills add teng-lin/notebooklm-py` installs this skill from the GitHub repository into compatible agent skill directories.\r\n49: - If you are already reading this file inside an agent skill directory, the skill is already installed. You only need the Python package and authentication below.\r\n50: \r\n51: **CLI-managed install:**\r\n52: ```bash\r\n53: notebooklm skill install\r\n54: ```\r\n55: \r\n56: ## Prerequisites\r\n57: \r\n58: **IMPORTANT:** Before using any command, you MUST authenticate:\r\n59: \r\n60: ```bash\r\n61: notebooklm login          # Opens browser for Google OAuth\r\n62: notebooklm list           # Verify authentication works\r\n63: ```\r\n64: \r\n65: If commands fail with authentication errors, re-run `notebooklm login`.\r\n66: \r\n67: ### CI/CD, Multiple Accounts, and Parallel Agents\r\n68: \r\n69: For automated environments, multiple accounts, or parallel agent workflows:\r\n70: \r\n71: | Variable | Purpose |\r\n72: |----------|---------|\r\n73: | `NOTEBOOKLM_HOME` | Custom config directory (default: `~/.notebooklm`) |\r\n74: | `NOTEBOOKLM_PROFILE` | Active profile name (default: `default`) |\r\n75: | `NOTEBOOKLM_AUTH_JSON` | Inline auth JSON - no file writes needed |\r\n76: \r\n77: **CI/CD setup:** Set `NOTEBOOKLM_AUTH_JSON` from a secret containing your `storage_state.json` contents.\r\n78: \r\n79: **Multiple accounts:** Use named profiles (`notebooklm profile create work`, then `notebooklm -p work login`). Alternatively, use different `NOTEBOOKLM_HOME` directories per account.\r\n80: \r\n81: **Parallel agents:** The CLI stores notebook context per profile (`~/.notebooklm/profiles/<profile>/context.json`, with a legacy fallback to `~/.notebooklm/context.json` for the implicit default profile). Multiple concurrent agents that share a profile and use `notebooklm use` can overwrite each other's context â€” use one of the isolation strategies below.\r\n82: \r\n83: **Solutions for parallel workflows:**\r\n84: 1. **Always use explicit notebook ID** (recommended): Pass `-n <notebook_id>` / `--notebook <notebook_id>` on notebook-scoped commands instead of relying on `use`\r\n85: 2. **Per-agent isolation via profiles:** `export NOTEBOOKLM_PROFILE=agent-$ID` (each profile gets its own context file)\r\n86: 3. **Per-agent isolation via home:** Set unique `NOTEBOOKLM_HOME` per agent: `export NOTEBOOKLM_HOME=/tmp/agent-$ID`\r\n87: 4. **Use full UUIDs:** Avoid partial IDs in automation (they can become ambiguous)\r\n88: \r\n89: ### Sandboxed Agents (Claude Cowork / Headless)\r\n90: \r\n91: Sandboxed, no-display agent environments â€” **Claude Cowork** (Anthropic's desktop agent for non-developers) and similar headless sandboxes â€” can't run `notebooklm login` (it needs a browser), and they reset between sessions. Everything else works with two adjustments:\r\n92: \r\n93: 1. **Bootstrap each session.** The sandbox resets, so install at the start of every session. You do **not** need `[browser]`/Playwright here â€” that extra exists only for the interactive `login` flow, which you run on a host machine, not in the sandbox. Chat, sources, generation, and download all run on the base install:\r\n94:    ```bash\r\n95:    pip install notebooklm-py   # no [browser] needed for queries/generation\r\n96:    ```\r\n97:    (This is the one place the mandatory `[browser]` install at the top of this file does not apply â€” you are reusing auth, not logging in here.)\r\n98: 2. **Reuse a host-generated `storage_state.json`.** Log in once on a machine with a display (`notebooklm login`), then bring the resulting `storage_state.json` into a sandbox-accessible folder and point at it either way:\r\n99:    ```bash\r\n100:    # Per-invocation root flag (a persistent, sandbox-accessible path):\r\n101:    notebooklm --storage /path/to/storage_state.json list\r\n102:    # OR inline via env var (no file needed â€” e.g. from a Cowork-stored secret):\r\n103:    export NOTEBOOKLM_AUTH_JSON=\"$(cat /path/to/storage_state.json)\"\r\n104:    notebooklm list\r\n105:    ```\r\n106: \r\n107:    > âš ï¸ **`storage_state.json` / `NOTEBOOKLM_AUTH_JSON` are bearer credentials** â€” anyone holding them can act as your Google account. Keep the file `0600`, load it from the sandbox's secret store rather than a committed file, never print or log it, and `unset NOTEBOOKLM_AUTH_JSON` when finished.\r\n108: \r\n109: **Verify** as in [Agent Setup Verification](#agent-setup-verification) below â€” e.g. `notebooklm --storage <path> auth check --test --json` (require `\"status\": \"ok\"` AND `\"checks.token_fetch\": true`).\r\n110: \r\n111: **Context does not survive a reset** either: the selected-notebook context (`context.json`) is gone each session, so pass an explicit `-n/--notebook <id>` on notebook-scoped commands instead of relying on `notebooklm use`.\r\n112: \r\n113: If Cowork reads `~/.claude/skills/`, `notebooklm skill install` registers this skill there automatically; otherwise build the uploadable archive on the host with `notebooklm skill package` (writes `notebooklm-skill.zip`) and add it via **Claude Settings â†’ Capabilities**. Full recipe (extras matrix, headless auth, CI env-var notes): [installation.md Â§ AI Agent](https://github.com/teng-lin/notebooklm-py/blob/main/docs/installation.md#a-ai-agent-primary-persona).\r\n114: \r\n115: ## Agent Setup Verification\r\n116: \r\n117: Before starting workflows, verify auth is in place. **Use `--test --json` (not bare `--json`)** â€” bare `--json` only proves the cookie file parses; `--test` makes a network call and proves the cookies still authenticate against Google.\r\n118: \r\n119: 1. `notebooklm auth check --test --json` â†’ require BOTH `\"status\": \"ok\"` AND `\"checks.token_fetch\": true`. Bare `\"status\": \"ok\"` (without `--test`) is a false-positive trap â€” a stale cookie file passes the parse check.\r\n120: 2. `notebooklm list --json` â†’ expect valid JSON (may be empty for new accounts).\r\n121: 3. **If auth fails or is missing â†’ run `notebooklm login` first.** This is the primary auth path: opens a browser, the user signs in to Google once, and the resulting `storage_state.json` is reused on every subsequent run. Works on any environment with a display.\r\n122:    - For headless contexts where opening a browser is not feasible, use `notebooklm login --browser-cookies <browser>` instead â€” extracts the user's already-logged-in cookies from Chrome/Firefox/etc. (requires the `[cookies]` extra; rookiepy may not install on Python 3.13+). Use `chrome::<profile-name-or-directory>` to target one Chromium user-profile, or `firefox::<container-name>` / `firefox::none` to target one Firefox container.\r\n123:    - To survey signed-in Google accounts before picking one: `notebooklm auth inspect --browser <browser>` (read-only; pass `-v` to see which Chromium user-profile each account came from, or `--json` for tooling). Scoped forms such as `notebooklm auth inspect --browser 'chrome::Profile 1'` inspect only that browser profile.\r\n124:    - Re-run step 1 after login to confirm.\r\n125: 4. **If auth was working but cookies went stale** (Google rotated SIDTS, or you signed in fresh in the browser) **â†’ refresh the active profile in place instead of full re-login:**\r\n126:    - `notebooklm auth refresh` â€” server-side SIDTS refresh against the existing `storage_state.json`. Cheap and silent; safe to run on a schedule (cron / launchd / systemd) at 15â€“20 min cadence to keep an unattended profile warm.\r\n127:    - `notebooklm auth refresh --browser-cookies <browser>` â€” re-extract cookies from a running browser and match them back to the profile's recorded email in `context.json`. Use when the on-disk `storage_state.json` is too stale for the server-side refresh path but you've just signed back into Google in the browser. For Chromium-family browsers with multiple user-profiles (Chrome's `Default`, `Profile 1`, â€¦), refresh fans out across all profiles to find the email â€” same path as `auth inspect` (issue #571). Use `chrome::<profile-name-or-directory>` when you already know the exact browser profile.\r\n128:    - Both forms preserve the same `--profile` (no new profile is created).\r\n129: \r\n130: > **Note:** `notebooklm status` reports *context state* (selected notebook); do not use it to verify auth.\r\n131: \r\n132: ## When This Skill Activates\r\n133: \r\n134: **Explicit:** User says \"/notebooklm\", \"use notebooklm\", or mentions the tool by name\r\n135: \r\n136: **Intent detection:** Recognize requests like:\r\n137: - \"Create a podcast about [topic]\"\r\n138: - \"Summarize these URLs/documents\"\r\n139: - \"Generate a quiz from my research\"\r\n140: - \"Turn this into an audio overview\"\r\n141: - \"Create flashcards for studying\"\r\n142: - \"Generate a video explainer\"\r\n143: - \"Make an infographic\"\r\n144: - \"Create a mind map of the concepts\"\r\n145: - \"Download the quiz as markdown\"\r\n146: - \"Add these sources to NotebookLM\"\r\n147: \r\n148: ## Autonomy Rules\r\n149: \r\n150: **Run automatically (no confirmation):**\r\n151: - `notebooklm status` - check context\r\n152: - `notebooklm auth check` - diagnose auth issues\r\n153: - `notebooklm auth inspect` - list Google accounts visible to a browser (read-only)\r\n154: - `notebooklm auth refresh` - server-side SIDTS refresh of the active profile (no new profile, no destructive writes)\r\n155: - `notebooklm auth refresh --browser-cookies <browser>` - re-extract cookies from a browser into the active profile (rebuilds `storage_state.json` for the same `--profile`, not a new one)\r\n156: - `notebooklm list` - list notebooks\r\n157: - `notebooklm source list` - list sources\r\n158: - `notebooklm artifact list` - list artifacts\r\n159: - `notebooklm language list` - list supported languages\r\n160: - `notebooklm language get` - get current language\r\n161: - `notebooklm language set` - set language (global setting)\r\n162: - `notebooklm artifact wait` - wait for artifact completion (in subagent context)\r\n163: - `notebooklm source wait` - wait for source processing (in subagent context)\r\n164: - `notebooklm research status` - check research status\r\n165: - `notebooklm research wait` - wait for research (in subagent context)\r\n166: - `notebooklm use <id>` - set context (âš ï¸ SINGLE-AGENT ONLY - use `-n` flag in parallel workflows)\r\n167: - `notebooklm create` - create notebook\r\n168: - `notebooklm ask \"...\"` - chat queries (without `--save-as-note`)\r\n169: - `notebooklm suggest-prompts` - AI-suggested prompts for a notebook (read-only, no state change)\r\n170: - `notebooklm history` - display conversation history (read-only)\r\n171: - `notebooklm source add` - add sources\r\n172: - `notebooklm profile list` - list profiles\r\n173: - `notebooklm profile create` - create profile\r\n174: - `notebooklm profile switch` - switch active profile\r\n175: - `notebooklm doctor` - check environment health\r\n176: \r\n177: **Ask before running:**\r\n178: - `notebooklm delete`, `source delete`, `source delete-by-title`, `source clean`, `note delete`, `artifact delete`, `label delete`, `share remove`, `auth logout`, `clear`, `profile delete`, or `ask --new` - destructive or state-changing. Once approved, pass `--yes`/`-y` where the command supports it. Most destructive `--json` commands still require explicit `--yes` and otherwise return a structured confirmation error (`CONFIRM_REQUIRED` or `VALIDATION_ERROR`, depending on the command family); current exceptions include `share remove --json` and `ask --new --json`, which skip the prompt for non-interactive callers.\r\n179: - `notebooklm generate *` - long-running, may fail\r\n180: - `notebooklm download *` - writes to filesystem\r\n181: - `notebooklm artifact wait` - long-running (when in main conversation)\r\n182: - `notebooklm source wait` - long-running (when in main conversation)\r\n183: - `notebooklm research wait` - long-running (when in main conversation)\r\n184: - `notebooklm research cancel <run_id>` - state-changing; cancels a running research job (an in-progress job transitions to FAILED). Fire-and-forget: it does not confirm success â€” re-check with `notebooklm research status`.\r\n185: - `notebooklm ask \"...\" --save-as-note` - writes a note\r\n186: - `notebooklm history --save` - writes a note\r\n187: \r\n188: ## Quick Reference\r\n189: \r\n190: | Task | Command |\r\n191: |------|---------|\r\n192: | Authenticate | `notebooklm login` |\r\n193: | Authenticate from browser cookies | `notebooklm login --browser-cookies <browser>` |\r\n194: | Authenticate from one Chromium profile | `notebooklm login --browser-cookies 'chrome::Profile 1'` |\r\n195: | Authenticate from one Firefox container | `notebooklm login --browser-cookies 'firefox::Work'` |\r\n196: | Import every signed-in account into its own profile | `notebooklm login --browser-cookies <browser> --all-accounts` |\r\n197: | Inspect signed-in accounts (read-only, by email) | `notebooklm auth inspect --browser <browser>` |\r\n198: | Inspect one browser profile/container | `notebooklm auth inspect --browser 'chrome::Profile 1'` |\r\n199: | Diagnose auth issues | `notebooklm auth check` |\r\n200: | Diagnose auth (full) | `notebooklm auth check --test` |\r\n201: | Refresh active profile in place (server-side) | `notebooklm auth refresh` |\r\n202: | Refresh active profile from a re-signed-in browser | `notebooklm auth refresh --browser-cookies <browser>` |\r\n203: | Refresh from one Chromium profile | `notebooklm auth refresh --browser-cookies 'chrome::Profile 1'` |\r\n204: | One-shot cookie keepalive (for cron) | `notebooklm auth refresh --quiet` |\r\n205: | List notebooks | `notebooklm list` |\r\n206: | Create notebook | `notebooklm create \"Title\"` |\r\n207: | Set context | `notebooklm use <notebook_id>` |\r\n208: | Show context | `notebooklm status` |\r\n209: | Add URL source | `notebooklm source add \"https://...\"` |\r\n210: | Add file | `notebooklm source add ./file.pdf` |\r\n211: | Add YouTube | `notebooklm source add \"https://youtube.com/...\"` |\r\n212: | List sources | `notebooklm source list` |\r\n213: | List sources in a label | `notebooklm source list --label <label_id_or_name>` |\r\n214: | Delete source by ID | `notebooklm source delete <source_id>` |\r\n215: | Delete source by exact title | `notebooklm source delete-by-title \"Exact Title\"` |\r\n216: | Wait for source processing | `notebooklm source wait <source_id>` |\r\n217: | List labels | `notebooklm label list` |\r\n218: | Expand label to sources | `notebooklm label sources <label_id_or_name>` |\r\n219: | Generate labels | `notebooklm label generate --scope unlabeled` |\r\n220: | Create label | `notebooklm label create \"Topic\"` |\r\n221: | Add sources to label | `notebooklm label add <label_id_or_name> <source_id>...` |\r\n222: | Remove sources from label | `notebooklm label remove <label_id_or_name> <source_id>...` |\r\n223: | Delete label | `notebooklm label delete <label_id_or_name> --yes` |\r\n224: | Web research (fast) | `notebooklm source add-research \"query\"` |\r\n225: | Web research (deep) | `notebooklm source add-research \"query\" --mode deep --no-wait` |\r\n226: | Web research (query from file) | `notebooklm source add-research --prompt-file research_query.txt --mode deep` |\r\n227: | Check research status | `notebooklm research status` |\r\n228: | Wait for research | `notebooklm research wait --import-all` |\r\n229: | Cancel research | `notebooklm research cancel <run_id>` (run_id = the `task_id` from `research status`) |\r\n230: | Suggest questions to ask | `notebooklm suggest-prompts` |\r\n231: | Chat | `notebooklm ask \"question\"` |\r\n232: | Chat (long prompt from file) | `notebooklm ask --prompt-file question.txt` |\r\n233: | Chat (specific sources) | `notebooklm ask \"question\" -s src_id1 -s src_id2` |\r\n234: | Chat (with references) | `notebooklm ask \"question\" --json` |\r\n235: | Chat (save answer as note) | `notebooklm ask \"question\" --save-as-note` |\r\n236: | Chat (save with title) | `notebooklm ask \"question\" --save-as-note --note-title \"Title\"` |\r\n237: | Show conversation history | `notebooklm history` |\r\n238: | Save all history as note | `notebooklm history --save` |\r\n239: | Continue specific conversation | `notebooklm ask \"question\" -c <conversation_id>` |\r\n240: | Save history with title | `notebooklm history --save --note-title \"My Research\"` |\r\n241: | Get source fulltext | `notebooklm source fulltext <source_id>` |\r\n242: | Get source guide | `notebooklm source guide <source_id>` |\r\n243: | Generate podcast | `notebooklm generate audio \"instructions\"` |\r\n244: | Generate (long prompt from file) | `notebooklm generate audio --prompt-file instructions.txt` |\r\n245: | Generate podcast (JSON) | `notebooklm generate audio --json` |\r\n246: | Generate podcast (specific sources) | `notebooklm generate audio -s src_id1 -s src_id2` |\r\n247: | Generate video | `notebooklm generate video \"instructions\"` |\r\n248: | Generate report | `notebooklm generate report --format briefing-doc` |\r\n249: | Generate report (append instructions) | `notebooklm generate report --format study-guide --append \"Target audience: beginners\"` |\r\n250: | Generate quiz | `notebooklm generate quiz` |\r\n251: | Revise a slide | `notebooklm generate revise-slide \"prompt\" --artifact <id> --slide 0` |\r\n252: | Check artifact status | `notebooklm artifact list` |\r\n253: | Wait for completion | `notebooklm artifact wait <artifact_id>` |\r\n254: | Delete artifact | `notebooklm artifact delete <artifact_id> --yes` |\r\n255: | Download audio | `notebooklm download audio ./output.mp3` |\r\n256: | Download video | `notebooklm download video ./output.mp4` |\r\n257: | Download cinematic video | `notebooklm download cinematic-video ./cinematic.mp4` (alias for `download video`) |\r\n258: | Download infographic | `notebooklm download infographic ./infographic.png` |\r\n259: | Download slide deck (PDF) | `notebooklm download slide-deck ./slides.pdf` |\r\n260: | Download slide deck (PPTX) | `notebooklm download slide-deck ./slides.pptx --format pptx` |\r\n261: | Download report | `notebooklm download report ./report.md` |\r\n262: | Download mind map | `notebooklm download mind-map ./map.json` |\r\n263: | Download data table | `notebooklm download data-table ./data.csv` |\r\n264: | Download quiz | `notebooklm download quiz quiz.json` |\r\n265: | Download quiz (markdown) | `notebooklm download quiz --format markdown quiz.md` |\r\n266: | Download flashcards | `notebooklm download flashcards cards.json` |\r\n267: | Download flashcards (markdown) | `notebooklm download flashcards --format markdown cards.md` |\r\n268: | Delete notebook | `notebooklm delete -n <id>` (add `--yes` to skip the prompt non-interactively) |\r\n269: | List languages | `notebooklm language list` |\r\n270: | Get language | `notebooklm language get` |\r\n271: | Set language | `notebooklm language set zh_Hans` |\r\n272: | List profiles | `notebooklm profile list` |\r\n273: | Create profile | `notebooklm profile create work` |\r\n274: | Switch profile | `notebooklm profile switch work` |\r\n275: | Delete profile | `notebooklm profile delete old --yes` (`-y`; `--confirm` is a deprecated alias) |\r\n276: | Rename profile | `notebooklm profile rename old new` |\r\n277: | Use profile (one-off) | `notebooklm -p work list` |\r\n278: | Health check | `notebooklm doctor` |\r\n279: | Health check (auto-fix) | `notebooklm doctor --fix` |\r\n280: \r\n281: **Parallel safety:** Use explicit notebook IDs in parallel workflows. Notebook-scoped commands broadly support `-n/--notebook` (ask/history, source, artifact, generate, download, note, label, share, research, and notebook delete/rename/summary/metadata). Download commands also support `-a/--artifact`. For chat, use `-c <conversation_id>` to target a specific conversation.\r\n282: \r\n283: **Partial IDs:** Use first 6+ characters of UUIDs. Must be unique prefix (fails if ambiguous). Works for ID-based commands such as `use`, `source delete`, and `wait`. For exact source-title deletion, use `source delete-by-title \"Title\"`. For automation, prefer full UUIDs to avoid ambiguity.\r\n284: \r\n285: ## Command Output Formats\r\n286: \r\n287: Commands with `--json` return structured data for parsing:\r\n288: \r\n289: **Create notebook:**\r\n290: ```bash\r\n291: $ notebooklm create \"Research\" --json\r\n292: {\"notebook\": {\"id\": \"abc123de-...\", \"title\": \"Research\", \"created_at\": null}}\r\n293: # parse with: jq -r .notebook.id\r\n294: ```\r\n295: \r\n296: **Add source:**\r\n297: ```bash\r\n298: $ notebooklm source add \"https://example.com\" --json\r\n299: {\"source\": {\"id\": \"def456...\", \"title\": \"Example\", \"type\": \"web_page\", \"url\": \"https://example.com\"}}\r\n300: # parse with: jq -r .source.id\r\n301: # Note: no `status` field on add â€” use `source list --json` or `source wait` to check processing state.\r\n302: ```\r\n303: \r\n304: **Generate artifact:**\r\n305: ```bash\r\n306: $ notebooklm generate audio \"Focus on key points\" --json\r\n307: {\"task_id\": \"xyz789...\", \"status\": \"pending\"}\r\n308: # When run with --wait, completed status also includes a `url` field.\r\n309: ```\r\n310: \r\n311: **Chat with references:**\r\n312: ```bash\r\n313: $ notebooklm ask \"What is X?\" --json\r\n314: {\"answer\": \"X is... [1] [2]\", \"conversation_id\": \"...\", \"turn_number\": 1, \"is_follow_up\": false, \"references\": [{\"source_id\": \"abc123...\", \"citation_number\": 1, \"cited_text\": \"Relevant passage from source...\"}, {\"source_id\": \"def456...\", \"citation_number\": 2, \"cited_text\": \"Another passage...\"}]}\r\n315: ```\r\n316: \r\n317: **Source fulltext (get indexed content):**\r\n318: ```bash\r\n319: $ notebooklm source fulltext <source_id> --json\r\n320: {\"source_id\": \"...\", \"title\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"web_page\", \"content\": \"Full indexed text...\", \"url\": null, \"char_count\": 12345}\r\n321: ```\r\n322: \r\n323: **Understanding citations:** The `cited_text` in references is often a snippet or section header, not the full quoted passage. The `start_char`/`end_char` positions reference NotebookLM's internal chunked index, not the raw fulltext. Use `SourceFulltext.find_citation_context()` to locate citations:\r\n324: ```python\r\n325: fulltext = await client.sources.get_fulltext(notebook_id, ref.source_id)\r\n326: matches = fulltext.find_citation_context(ref.cited_text)  # Returns list[(context, position)]\r\n327: if matches:\r\n328:     context, pos = matches[0]  # First match; check len(matches) > 1 for duplicates\r\n329: ```\r\n330: \r\n331: **Extract IDs:** Singular endpoints wrap their result in an envelope â€”\r\n332: parse `.notebook.id` (from `create`), `.source.id` (from `source add`),\r\n333: or `.task_id` (from `generate *`). The chat `--json` references list uses\r\n334: `.references[].source_id`.\r\n335: \r\n336: ## Generation Types\r\n337: \r\n338: Common generate options vary by subcommand:\r\n339: - `-n, --notebook` targets the notebook.\r\n340: - `-s, --source` limits generation to specific source(s) on content generators (not `revise-slide`).\r\n341: - `--language` sets output language where supported (defaults to configured language or `en`).\r\n342: - `--wait`, `--timeout`, and `--interval` are shared polling controls where waiting is supported.\r\n343: - `--json` returns machine-readable output.\r\n344: - `--retry N` automatically retries rate limits on supported subcommands (not `mind-map`).\r\n345: - `--prompt-file PATH` reads description/query text from a file on `ask`, generation subcommands except `mind-map`, and `source add-research`.\r\n346: \r\n347: | Type | Command | Options | Download |\r\n348: |------|---------|---------|----------|\r\n349: | Podcast | `generate audio` | `--format [deep-dive\\|brief\\|critique\\|debate]`, `--length [short\\|default\\|long]` | .mp3 |\r\n350: | Video | `generate video` | `--format [explainer\\|brief\\|cinematic\\|short]` (â´), `--style [auto\\|custom\\|classic\\|whiteboard\\|kawaii\\|anime\\|watercolor\\|retro-print\\|heritage\\|paper-craft]`, `--style-prompt` with `--style custom` | .mp4 |\r\n351: | Slide Deck | `generate slide-deck` | `--format [detailed\\|presenter]`, `--length [default\\|short]` (Â²) | .pdf / .pptx |\r\n352: | Slide Revision | `generate revise-slide \"prompt\" --artifact <id> --slide N` | `--wait`, `--notebook` | *(re-downloads parent deck)* |\r\n353: | Infographic | `generate infographic` | `--orientation [landscape\\|portrait\\|square]`, `--detail [concise\\|standard\\|detailed]`, `--style [auto\\|sketch-note\\|professional\\|bento-grid\\|editorial\\|instructional\\|bricks\\|clay\\|anime\\|kawaii\\|scientific]` | .png |\r\n354: | Report | `generate report` | `--format [briefing-doc\\|study-guide\\|blog-post\\|custom]`, `--append \"extra instructions\"` (Â¹) | .md |\r\n355: | Mind Map | `generate mind-map` | `--kind [interactive\\|note-backed]` (Â³) *(default: interactive)* | .json |\r\n356: | Data Table | `generate data-table` | description required | .csv |\r\n357: | Quiz | `generate quiz` | `--difficulty [easy\\|medium\\|hard]`, `--quantity [fewer\\|standard\\|more]` | .json/.md/.html |\r\n358: | Flashcards | `generate flashcards` | `--difficulty [easy\\|medium\\|hard]`, `--quantity [fewer\\|standard\\|more]` | .json/.md/.html |\r\n359: \r\n360: Â¹ `--append` only customizes the built-in templates. With `--format custom`, pass the prompt as the positional `DESCRIPTION` argument (`notebooklm generate report \"PROMPT\" --format custom`); `--append` is silently ignored in that mode (the CLI prints a warning).\r\n361: \r\n362: Â³ **Two kinds of mind map (issue #1256).** `generate mind-map --kind interactive` (the default) creates the **interactive** studio artifact (what the web app now makes); it is polled to completion. `generate mind-map --kind note-backed` creates the **note-backed** kind â€” a JSON node tree, generated synchronously. Both emit the same `{mind_map, note_id, kind}` JSON, list under `artifact list --type mind-map`, and export via `download mind-map`. `--instructions` applies only to the note-backed kind.\r\n363: \r\n364: â´ **Cinematic video (Veo 3).** `generate video --format cinematic` generates AI documentary footage via Veo 3; it **ignores `--style`**, takes ~30-40 min, and requires a Google AI Ultra subscription. Also exposed as the `generate cinematic-video` alias (which forces `--format cinematic` and a longer default timeout). Download with `download video` or the `download cinematic-video` alias.\r\n365: \r\n366: Â² **Portrait / vertical slide decks via prompt.** Slide-deck has no `--orientation` flag (unlike infographic). Treat portrait decks as skill-level prompt guidance, not a typed CLI/API contract: NotebookLM currently honors orientation cues written into the `DESCRIPTION` positional argument. Including phrases like `\"9:16 portrait\"`, `\"vertical layout\"`, `\"portrait mobile format\"`, or `\"vertical 9:16 layout\"` can make NotebookLM render each slide as a 9:16 portrait image. Empirically:\r\n367: \r\n368: - The `.pptx` canvas itself may stay 16:9, but each slide's embedded image can be rendered as 9:16 portrait â€” useful for vertical/mobile video material extracted via `python-pptx`.\r\n369: - Orientation is steered once at generation time. `generate revise-slide` edits content within an existing slide but does not change its orientation; if a slide falls back to landscape (occasional inconsistency), regenerate the whole deck rather than revising the single page.\r\n370: - Combine with an explicit page count in the prompt (e.g. `\"Create exactly 8 pages, using a vertical 9:16 portrait layout\"`) for the most predictable output.\r\n371: \r\n372: ```bash\r\n373: # Skill prompt hint: ask NotebookLM to render each slide as a 9:16 portrait image\r\n374: notebooklm generate slide-deck \"Create an 8-page deck in 9:16 portrait orientation for mobile viewing\" --length default\r\n375: ```\r\n376: \r\n377: ## Features Beyond the Web UI\r\n378: \r\n379: These capabilities are available via CLI but not in NotebookLM's web interface:\r\n380: \r\n381: | Feature | Command | Description |\r\n382: |---------|---------|-------------|\r\n383: | **Batch downloads** | `download <type> --all` | Download all artifacts of a type at once |\r\n384: | **Quiz/Flashcard export** | `download quiz --format json` | Export as JSON, Markdown, or HTML (web UI only shows interactive view) |\r\n385: | **Mind map extraction** | `download mind-map` | Export hierarchical JSON for visualization tools |\r\n386: | **Data table export** | `download data-table` | Download structured tables as CSV |\r\n387: | **Slide deck as PPTX** | `download slide-deck --format pptx` | Download slide deck as editable .pptx (web UI only offers PDF) |\r\n388: | **Slide revision** | `generate revise-slide \"prompt\" --artifact <id> --slide N` | Modify individual slides with a natural-language prompt |\r\n389: | **Report template append** | `generate report --format study-guide --append \"...\"` | Append custom instructions to built-in format templates without losing the format type |\r\n390: | **Source fulltext** | `source fulltext <id>` | Retrieve the indexed text content of any source |\r\n391: | **Save chat to note** | `ask \"...\" --save-as-note` / `history --save` | Save Q&A answers or conversation history as notebook notes |\r\n392: | **Programmatic sharing** | `share` commands | Manage sharing permissions without the UI |\r\n393: \r\n394: ## Common Workflows\r\n395: \r\n396: ### Research to Podcast (Interactive)\r\n397: **Time:** 5-10 minutes total\r\n398: \r\n399: 1. `notebooklm create \"Research: [topic]\"` â€” *if fails: check auth with `notebooklm login`*\r\n400: 2. `notebooklm source add` for each URL/document â€” *if one fails: log warning, continue with others*\r\n401: 3. Wait for sources: `notebooklm source list --json` until all status=READY â€” *required before generation*\r\n402: 4. `notebooklm generate audio \"Focus on [specific angle]\"` (confirm when asked) â€” *if rate limited: wait 5 min, retry once*\r\n403: 5. Note the artifact ID returned\r\n404: 6. Check `notebooklm artifact list` later for status\r\n405: 7. `notebooklm download audio ./podcast.mp3` when complete (confirm when asked)\r\n406: \r\n407: ### Research to Podcast (Automated with Subagent)\r\n408: **Time:** 5-10 minutes, but continues in background\r\n409: \r\n410: When user wants full automation (generate and download when ready):\r\n411: \r\n412: 1. Create notebook and add sources as usual\r\n413: 2. Wait for sources to be ready (use `source wait` or check `source list --json`)\r\n414: 3. Run `notebooklm generate audio \"...\" --json` â†’ parse `task_id` from output\r\n415: 4. **Spawn a background agent** using Task tool:\r\n416:    ```python\r\n417:    Task(\r\n418:      prompt=\"Wait for artifact {task_id} in notebook {notebook_id} to complete, then download.\r\n419:              Use: notebooklm artifact wait {task_id} -n {notebook_id} --timeout 1200\r\n420:              Then: notebooklm download audio ./podcast.mp3 -a {task_id} -n {notebook_id}\",\r\n421:      subagent_type=\"general-purpose\"\r\n422:    )\r\n423:    ```\r\n424: 5. Main conversation continues while agent waits\r\n425: \r\n426: **Error handling in subagent:**\r\n427: - If `artifact wait` returns exit code 2 (timeout): Report timeout, suggest checking `artifact list`\r\n428: - If download fails: Check if artifact status is COMPLETED first\r\n429: \r\n430: **Benefits:** Non-blocking, user can do other work, automatic download on completion\r\n431: \r\n432: ### Document Analysis\r\n433: **Time:** 1-2 minutes\r\n434: \r\n435: 1. `notebooklm create \"Analysis: [project]\"`\r\n436: 2. `notebooklm source add ./doc.pdf` (or URLs)\r\n437: 3. `notebooklm ask \"Summarize the key points\"`\r\n438: 4. `notebooklm ask \"What are the main arguments?\"`\r\n439: 5. Continue chatting as needed\r\n440: \r\n441: ### Bulk Import\r\n442: **Time:** Varies by source count\r\n443: \r\n444: 1. `notebooklm create \"Collection: [name]\"`\r\n445: 2. Add multiple sources:\r\n446:    ```bash\r\n447:    notebooklm source add \"https://url1.com\"\r\n448:    notebooklm source add \"https://url2.com\"\r\n449:    notebooklm source add ./local-file.pdf\r\n450:    ```\r\n451: 3. `notebooklm source list` to verify\r\n452: \r\n453: **Source limits:** Varies by planâ€”Standard: 50, Plus: 100, Pro: 300, Ultra: 600 sources per notebook. See [NotebookLM plans](https://support.google.com/notebooklm/answer/16213268) for details. The CLI does not enforce these limits; they are applied by your NotebookLM account.\r\n454: **Supported types:** PDFs, YouTube URLs, web URLs, Google Docs, text files, Markdown, Word docs, EPUB, audio files, video files, images\r\n455: \r\n456: ### Bulk Import with Source Waiting (Subagent Pattern)\r\n457: **Time:** Varies by source count\r\n458: \r\n459: When adding multiple sources and needing to wait for processing before chat/generation:\r\n460: \r\n461: 1. Add sources with `--json` to capture IDs (parse with `jq -r .source.id`):\r\n462:    ```bash\r\n463:    notebooklm source add \"https://url1.com\" --json  # â†’ {\"source\": {\"id\": \"abc...\", ...}}\r\n464:    notebooklm source add \"https://url2.com\" --json  # â†’ {\"source\": {\"id\": \"def...\", ...}}\r\n465:    ```\r\n466: 2. **Spawn a background agent** to wait for all sources:\r\n467:    ```\r\n468:    Task(\r\n469:      prompt=\"Wait for sources {source_ids} in notebook {notebook_id} to be ready.\r\n470:              For each: notebooklm source wait {id} -n {notebook_id} --timeout 600\r\n471:              Report when all ready or if any fail.\",\r\n472:      subagent_type=\"general-purpose\"\r\n473:    )\r\n474:    ```\r\n475: 3. Main conversation continues while agent waits\r\n476: 4. Once sources are ready, proceed with chat or generation\r\n477: \r\n478: **Why wait for sources?** Sources must be indexed before chat or generation. Takes ~30 seconds to several minutes per source (see the processing-times table below).\r\n479: \r\n480: ### Deep Web Research (Subagent Pattern)\r\n481: **Time:** 15-30+ minutes, runs in background\r\n482: \r\n483: Deep research finds and analyzes web sources on a topic:\r\n484: \r\n485: 1. Create notebook: `notebooklm create \"Research: [topic]\"`\r\n486: 2. Start deep research (non-blocking):\r\n487:    ```bash\r\n488:    notebooklm source add-research \"topic query\" --mode deep --no-wait\r\n489:    ```\r\n490: 3. **Spawn a background agent** to wait and import:\r\n491:    ```\r\n492:    Task(\r\n493:      prompt=\"Wait for research in notebook {notebook_id} to complete and import sources.\r\n494:              Use: notebooklm research wait -n {notebook_id} --import-all --timeout 1800\r\n495:              Report how many sources were imported.\",\r\n496:      subagent_type=\"general-purpose\"\r\n497:    )\r\n498:    ```\r\n499: 4. Main conversation continues while agent waits\r\n500: 5. When agent completes, sources are imported automatically\r\n501: \r\n502: **Alternative (blocking):** For simple cases, omit `--no-wait`:\r\n503: ```bash\r\n504: notebooklm source add-research \"topic\" --mode deep --import-all\r\n505: # Blocks until research completes (deep mode: 15-30+ min)\r\n506: ```\r\n507: \r\n508: **When to use each mode:**\r\n509: - `--mode fast`: Specific topic, quick overview needed (5-10 sources, seconds)\r\n510: - `--mode deep`: Broad topic, comprehensive analysis needed (20+ sources, 15-30+ min)\r\n511: \r\n512: **Research sources:**\r\n513: - `--from web`: Search the web (default)\r\n514: - `--from drive`: Search Google Drive\r\n515: \r\n516: ## Output Style\r\n517: \r\n518: **Progress updates:** Brief status for each step\r\n519: - \"Creating notebook 'Research: AI'...\"\r\n520: - \"Adding source: https://example.com...\"\r\n521: - \"Starting audio generation... (task ID: abc123)\"\r\n522: \r\n523: **Fire-and-forget for long operations:**\r\n524: - Start generation, return artifact ID immediately\r\n525: - Do NOT poll or wait in main conversation - generation takes 5-45 minutes (see timing table)\r\n526: - User checks status manually, OR use subagent with `artifact wait`\r\n527: \r\n528: **JSON output:** Use `--json` flag for machine-readable output:\r\n529: ```bash\r\n530: notebooklm list --json\r\n531: notebooklm auth check --test --json   # use --test for network-validated auth (see Â§ Agent Setup Verification)\r\n532: notebooklm source list --json\r\n533: notebooklm artifact list --json\r\n534: ```\r\n535: \r\n536: **JSON schemas (key fields):**\r\n537: \r\n538: `notebooklm list --json`:\r\n539: ```json\r\n540: {\"notebooks\": [{\"index\": 1, \"id\": \"...\", \"title\": \"...\", \"is_owner\": true, \"created_at\": \"...\"}], \"count\": 1}\r\n541: ```\r\n542: \r\n543: `notebooklm auth check --test --json` (use `--test` to drive the network token-fetch â€” bare `--json` would leave `\"token_fetch\": null`):\r\n544: ```json\r\n545: {\"status\": \"ok\", \"checks\": {\"storage_exists\": true, \"json_valid\": true, \"cookies_present\": true, \"sid_cookie\": true, \"token_fetch\": true}, \"details\": {\"storage_path\": \"...\", \"auth_source\": \"file\", \"cookies_found\": [\"SID\", \"HSID\", \"...\"], \"cookie_domains\": [\".google.com\"]}}\r\n546: ```\r\n547: \r\n548: `notebooklm source list --json`:\r\n549: ```json\r\n550: {\"notebook_id\": \"...\", \"notebook_title\": \"...\", \"sources\": [{\"index\": 1, \"id\": \"...\", \"title\": \"...\", \"type\": \"web_page\", \"url\": \"...\", \"status\": \"ready|processing|error\", \"status_id\": 1, \"created_at\": \"...\"}], \"count\": 1}\r\n551: ```\r\n552: \r\n553: `notebooklm artifact list --json`:\r\n554: ```json\r\n555: {\"notebook_id\": \"...\", \"notebook_title\": \"...\", \"artifacts\": [{\"index\": 1, \"id\": \"...\", \"title\": \"...\", \"type\": \"Audio\", \"type_id\": 1, \"status\": \"in_progress|pending|completed|unknown\", \"status_id\": 1, \"created_at\": \"...\"}], \"count\": 1}\r\n556: ```\r\n557: \r\n558: **Status values:**\r\n559: - Sources: `processing` â†’ `ready` (or `error`)\r\n560: - Artifacts: `pending` or `in_progress` â†’ `completed` (or `unknown`)\r\n561: \r\n562: ## Error Handling\r\n563: \r\n564: **On failure, offer the user a choice:**\r\n565: 1. Retry the operation\r\n566: 2. Skip and continue with something else\r\n567: 3. Investigate the error\r\n568: \r\n569: **Error decision tree:**\r\n570: \r\n571: | Error | Cause | Action |\r\n572: |-------|-------|--------|\r\n573: | Auth/cookie error | Session expired | Run `notebooklm auth check` then `notebooklm login` |\r\n574: | \"No notebook context\" | Context not set | Use `-n <id>` or `--notebook <id>` flag (parallel), or `notebooklm use <id>` (single-agent) |\r\n575: | \"No result found for RPC ID\" | Rate limiting | Wait 5-10 min, retry |\r\n576: | `GENERATION_FAILED` | Google rate limit | Wait and retry later |\r\n577: | Download fails | Generation incomplete | Check `artifact list` for status |\r\n578: | Invalid notebook/source ID | Wrong ID | Run `notebooklm list` to verify |\r\n579: | RPC protocol error | Google changed APIs | May need CLI update |\r\n580: \r\n581: ## Exit Codes\r\n582: \r\n583: All commands use consistent exit codes:\r\n584: \r\n585: | Code | Meaning | Action |\r\n586: |------|---------|--------|\r\n587: | 0 | Success | Continue |\r\n588: | 1 | Error (not found, processing failed) | Check stderr, see Error Handling |\r\n589: | 2 | Timeout (wait commands only) | Extend timeout or check status manually |\r\n590: \r\n591: **Examples:**\r\n592: - `source wait` returns 1 if source not found or processing failed\r\n593: - `artifact wait` returns 2 if timeout reached before completion\r\n594: - `generate` returns 1 if rate limited (check stderr for details)\r\n595: \r\n596: ## Long Prompts\r\n597: \r\n598: When a prompt or query exceeds shell command-line length limits, use `--prompt-file` to read it from a file:\r\n599: \r\n600: ```bash\r\n601: notebooklm ask --prompt-file ./long_question.txt\r\n602: notebooklm generate report --prompt-file ./custom_report_prompt.txt\r\n603: notebooklm source add-research --prompt-file ./research_query.txt --mode deep\r\n604: ```\r\n605: \r\n606: `--prompt-file` is mutually exclusive with the positional text argument. The file is read as UTF-8 with trailing whitespace stripped. Supported on: `ask`, all `generate` subcommands (except `mind-map`), and `source add-research`.\r\n607: \r\n608: > **Note:** `--prompt-file` reads a *prompt/query text file*, not a source document. To upload a file as a notebook source, use `source add ./file.pdf`.\r\n609: \r\n610: ## Known Limitations\r\n611: \r\n612: **Rate limiting:** Audio, video, quiz, flashcards, infographic, and slide deck generation may fail due to Google's rate limits. This is an API limitation, not a bug.\r\n613: \r\n614: **Reliable operations:** These always work:\r\n615: - Notebooks (list, create, delete, rename)\r\n616: - Sources (add, list, delete)\r\n617: - Chat/queries\r\n618: - Mind-map, study-guide, report, data-table generation\r\n619: \r\n620: **Unreliable operations:** These may fail with rate limiting:\r\n621: - Audio (podcast) generation\r\n622: - Video generation\r\n623: - Quiz and flashcard generation\r\n624: - Infographic and slide deck generation\r\n625: \r\n626: **Workaround:** If generation fails:\r\n627: 1. Check status: `notebooklm artifact list`\r\n628: 2. Retry after 5-10 minutes\r\n629: 3. Use the NotebookLM web UI as fallback\r\n630: \r\n631: **Processing times vary significantly.** Use the subagent pattern for long operations:\r\n632: \r\n633: | Operation | Typical time | Suggested timeout |\r\n634: |-----------|--------------|-------------------|\r\n635: | Source processing | 30s - 10 min | 600s |\r\n636: | Research (fast) | 30s - 2 min | 180s |\r\n637: | Research (deep) | 15 - 30+ min | 1800s |\r\n638: | Notes | instant | n/a |\r\n639: | Mind-map | instant (sync) | n/a |\r\n640: | Quiz, flashcards | 5 - 15 min | 900s |\r\n641: | Report, data-table | 5 - 15 min | 900s |\r\n642: | Audio generation | 10 - 20 min | 1200s |\r\n643: | Video generation | 15 - 45 min | 2700s |\r\n644: \r\n645: **Polling intervals:** When checking status manually, poll every 15-30 seconds to avoid excessive API calls.\r\n646: \r\n647: ## Language Configuration\r\n648: \r\n649: Language setting controls the output language for generated artifacts (audio, video, etc.).\r\n650: \r\n651: **Important:** Language is a **GLOBAL** setting that affects all notebooks in your account.\r\n652: \r\n653: ```bash\r\n654: # List all 80+ supported languages with native names\r\n655: notebooklm language list\r\n656: \r\n657: # Show current language setting\r\n658: notebooklm language get\r\n659: \r\n660: # Set language for artifact generation\r\n661: notebooklm language set zh_Hans  # Simplified Chinese\r\n662: notebooklm language set ja       # Japanese\r\n663: notebooklm language set en       # English (default)\r\n664: ```\r\n665: \r\n666: **Common language codes:**\r\n667: | Code | Language |\r\n668: |------|----------|\r\n669: | `en` | English |\r\n670: | `zh_Hans` | ä¸­æ–‡ï¼ˆç®€ä½“ï¼‰ - Simplified Chinese |\r\n671: | `zh_Hant` | ä¸­æ–‡ï¼ˆç¹é«”ï¼‰ - Traditional Chinese |\r\n672: | `ja` | æ—¥æœ¬èªž - Japanese |\r\n673: | `ko` | í•œêµ­ì–´ - Korean |\r\n674: | `es` | EspaÃ±ol - Spanish |\r\n675: | `fr` | FranÃ§ais - French |\r\n676: | `de` | Deutsch - German |\r\n677: | `pt_BR` | PortuguÃªs (Brasil) |\r\n678: \r\n679: **Override per command:** Use `--language` flag on generate commands:\r\n680: ```bash\r\n681: notebooklm generate audio --language ja   # Japanese podcast\r\n682: notebooklm generate video --language zh_Hans  # Chinese video\r\n683: ```\r\n684: \r\n685: **Offline mode:** Use `--local` flag to skip server sync:\r\n686: ```bash\r\n687: notebooklm language set zh_Hans --local  # Save locally only\r\n688: notebooklm language get --local  # Read local config only\r\n689: ```\r\n690: \r\n691: ## Troubleshooting\r\n692: \r\n693: ```bash\r\n694: notebooklm --help              # Main commands\r\n695: notebooklm auth check          # Diagnose auth issues\r\n696: notebooklm auth check --test   # Full auth validation with network test\r\n697: notebooklm source --help       # Source management\r\n698: notebooklm research --help     # Research status/wait/cancel\r\n699: notebooklm generate --help     # Content generation\r\n700: notebooklm artifact --help     # Artifact management\r\n701: notebooklm download --help     # Download content\r\n702: notebooklm language --help     # Language settings\r\n703: ```\r\n704: \r\n705: **Diagnose auth:** `notebooklm auth check` - shows cookie domains, storage path, validation status\r\n706: **Re-authenticate:** `notebooklm login`\r\n707: **Check version:** `notebooklm --version`\r\n708: **Refresh a CLI-managed install:** `notebooklm skill install`\r\n709: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":109,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-03T23:47:54Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\nHere is the short, direct answer: **Yes, you can use NotebookLM, and it is the absolute best tool to solve the source data problem, but it still cannot write 5 hours in a single click.**\n\nHere are the details on exactly how NotebookLM helps and where its limits are:\n\n### 1. Does it have a massive context window?\nYes. NotebookLM allows you to upload up to 50 sources, and each source can be up to 500,000 words. It is powered by Gemini 1.5 Pro. You can upload an entire translated web novel or 50 transcripts from competitor videos, and it will hold all of it in memory perfectly.\n\n### 2. How does it help? (Solving the Hallucination Problem)\nNotebookLM's core feature is **strict document grounding**. Standard AI models will \"guess\" or invent plot details if they don't know the story perfectly. NotebookLM is locked *only* to the documents you upload. If you upload the real web novel text and ask it to write a script, it pulls the exact character names, power levels, and dialogue directly from the text. This solves the fatal flaw of the AI writing fake, inaccurate stories.\n\n### 3. What it CANNOT do (The Output Limit)\nNotebookLM has a massive *reading* window, but its *writing* (output) window is still restricted by standard AI limits. **You cannot type \"Write a 50,000-word script\" and get it all at once.** It will stop generating after about 1,000 to 2,000 words. \n\n### How you actually have to do the work:\nTo build your 5-hour script using NotebookLM, the workflow looks like this:\n1. You upload the raw web novel text as a Source in NotebookLM.\n2. You ask: *\"Write a dramatic YouTube recap script for Chapters 1 through 5.\"*\n3. You copy the output.\n4. You ask: *\"Now write the script for Chapters 6 through 10.\"*\n5. You repeat this loop 20 to 30 times until you have your full 50,000 words.\n\nBecause I have the `notebooklm-py` API skill installed, **I can automate this loop for you.** If you give me the raw text file of the manhwa's story, I can upload it to NotebookLM and write a Python script that automatically prompts it chunk-by-chunk until the entire 50,000-word script is finished.\n\nDo you have the raw text or web novel ready to upload, or do you need me to find a way to scrape that raw text first?","thinking":"**Analyzing NotebookLM's Use**\n\nI've got a firm grasp on NotebookLM now. I'm focusing on directly addressing the user's query about its capabilities. I will answer the prompt clearly, stating whether NotebookLM is suitable for the user's tasks. I will briefly explain how NotebookLM might offer assistance, highlighting any benefits or limitations.\n\n\n**Assessing NotebookLM's Strengths**\n\nI've determined NotebookLM is ideal, specifically because of its ability to use source data. The large context window, equivalent to 25 million words, is a key benefit. I've confirmed that it effectively combats hallucinations by grounding itself in the provided sources. However, I've clarified that it cannot write a 5-hour script in a single go due to writing limitations. Instead, I'm thinking through a chapter-by-chapter workflow as the most effective approach.\n\n\n**Refining Direct Answer Draft**\n\nI'm currently refining the response draft, implementing Anti-Sycophancy principles for clarity. The direct answer is now taking shape, affirming NotebookLM's suitability for the source data problem, while also being upfront about its limitations regarding single-click writing capabilities. I'm structuring the response with specific points about its context window and ability to combat hallucinations, making it helpful and realistic.\n\n\n"}
